    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/wcd-7-393-2026",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Understanding the plausible upper bounds of extreme weather events is essential for risk assessment in a warming climate. Existing methods, based on large ensembles of physics-based models, are often computationally expensive or lack the fidelity needed to simulate rare, high-impact extremes. Here, we present a novel framework that leverages a differentiable hybrid climate model, NeuralGCM, to optimize initial conditions and generate physically consistent worst-case heatwave trajectories. Applied to the 2021 Pacific Northwest heatwave, our method produces heatwave intensity up to 3.7 \u00b0C above the most extreme member of a 75-member ensemble. These trajectories feature intensified atmospheric blocking and amplified Rossby wave patterns\u2014hallmarks of severe heat events. Our results demonstrate that differentiable climate models can efficiently explore the upper tails of event likelihoods, providing a powerful new approach for constructing targeted storylines of extreme weather under climate change.",
    "field": "Extreme event storylines",
    "problem": "Existing methods for assessing extreme weather events, which use large model ensembles, are computationally expensive and lack the fidelity needed to efficiently simulate rare, high-impact extremes and determine their plausible upper bounds.",
    "method": "The authors propose an optimization framework that uses a differentiable hybrid climate model to find initial condition perturbations that evolve into physically consistent, worst-case heatwave storylines, offering a computationally efficient alternative to large ensemble methods.",
    "task": "The task is to use a differentiable hybrid climate model to optimize initial conditions for generating physically consistent, worst-case trajectories or \"storylines\" of extreme heatwave events.",
